%% Intro diagram 2 — how one feature's record evolves (the README's checkout example).
%% EP-checkout and its fix EP-checkout-queue-filter are real epics in epics/. The queue-sort change is
%% the one the README demo adds (docs/media/feature-change.tape). Both later epics are children of the
%% feature. Rules drawn here (cli/epic-state.mjs resolveCurrentArtifacts): a later epic that re-writes
%% an artifact replaces it; an inherited artifact stays; stories add up, and `supersedes` retires a
%% parent story. Regenerate with `npm run diagrams`.
flowchart TD
    classDef knowledge fill:#fcf3cf,stroke:#b7950b,color:#000
    classDef work fill:#d6eaf8,stroke:#2471a3,color:#000
    classDef retired fill:#eaecee,stroke:#566573,color:#000,stroke-dasharray:5 3

    f["1 · Checkout feature<br/>epic, architecture,<br/>contract, UI, stories"]:::work
    d["2 · Fix: add the missing<br/>queue test<br/>new story, new tests"]:::work
    c["3 · Change: sort the queue<br/>new epic text<br/>replaces one story"]:::work

    keep["Still applies<br/>architecture · contract · UI<br/>other stories"]:::knowledge
    gone["Replaced or retired<br/>old epic text<br/>old queue story"]:::retired
    now[("Current picture<br/>for the next task")]:::knowledge

    f -->|fix builds on it| d
    f -->|change builds on it| c
    f -.->|still applies| keep
    c -.->|replaces| gone
    keep --> now
    d -->|story and tests add up| now
    c -->|latest epic text| now

    linkStyle default stroke:#8a8a8a,stroke-width:2px
